Chapter 9 is about archiving and compressing files in Linux.

Archiving combines multiple files/directories into one archive, mainly using tar.
Compression reduces file size to save storage and make transferring files faster.
Lossless compression keeps all the original information.
Lossy compression removes some information to achieve a smaller file size.
Common compression tools are gzip, bzip2, and xz.
tar has three important operations:
-c → Create
-t → List
-x → Extract
-z is used with gzip.
-j is used with bzip2.
-v means verbose and shows what is being processed.
-f specifies the archive filename.
Linux also supports ZIP files using zip and unzip.
zip -r is used to include directories and their contents.
unzip -l lists the contents of a ZIP file.
